{
  "id": "COMPOUND:14268 - PROTEIN:4629",
  "verbalization": "Benzocaine is implicated in the regulation of Non-receptor tyrosine-protein kinase TNK1 through a series of molecular interactions involving K-252a. The evidence suggests that benzocaine acts as a target of K-252a, which in turn targets TNK1. This pathway is supported by multiple independent connections, all of which point to a shared dependency on K-252a as a critical mediator. The presence of multiple targets—such as Glutamine--fructose-6-phosphate aminotransferase and Solute carrier organic anion transporter family member 2A1—indicates that K-252a may function as a central node in a broader regulatory network. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of K-252a in modulating TNK1 activity, potentially through the regulation of metabolic and transport processes. The consistent involvement of K-252a as a target of benzocaine and TNK1 underscores a recurring mechanism of action, where K-252a serves as a bridge between benzocaine and the kinase.",
